{
  "title": "上传与预审的历史检查记录",
  "date": "2026-09-04",
  "context": "真实设计稿的观察记录与固定合成答复分组保存。此处是历史文件的原值摘录，按原始文件名对应。",
  "instruction": {
    "revision": "1 → 2",
    "source": "candidate-skill/references/review-input.md",
    "text": "Before normalizing R4/R5 input, identify operations whose timing, completion, cancellation, or retry behavior may be answered independently. For example, uploading a file and validating it are separate operations; processing, failed, and successful screens for the same validation are states of one operation, not three operations.\n\nThe v0.1 engine does not scope async candidates by `uiFeatures.id`. Without confirmed transitions, multiple `async_action` features can collapse into one R4 candidate; shared prerequisite timing can also mix operations. Candidate-specific answers alone cannot recover distinctions already lost during normalization. Use one `ReviewRunInput` per independently answerable async operation:\n\n1. Assign a stable operation-specific `analysisId`, such as `review-material-upload` and `review-material-validation`, and an explicit `labels.actionLabel`.\n2. Include that operation's attributable features and evidence. Keep its related states together; reuse a frame across inputs when it contains different operations. Do not duplicate other operations' features or unconfirmed runtime facts into the scope.\n3. Keep unverified transitions absent. Frame order, adjacent placement, and similar names do not establish a transition. Splitting analysis scope does not require inventing one.\n4. Run each input, then attach only that operation's actual designer answers to the returned candidate IDs. Preserve a scope-to-source map outside the engine input; do not add unsupported schema fields.\n5. Combine the readable results with operation labels and all analysis/candidate IDs. Two R4.Q1 questions for different operations are not equivalent facts: ask each operation's timing explicitly, even if presented in one sentence. Never copy one operation's timing to another.\n\nThis is an R4/R5 normalization boundary, not an instruction to split every screen. Keep the shared-view/state evidence needed by R2/R6 in its own coherent review scope. If a large selection is sampled, state exactly which operations/screens were analyzed and which remain unreviewed.\n\nExample: a selected design shows upload controls plus a separate validation progress screen. With no runtime answers, run two inputs and expect two pending R4.Q1 questions, one per operation. Visible progress or result screens remain design evidence and do not supply designer timing answers. If multiple independent operations remain in an input, narrow the scope before interpreting or answering its combined candidate.\n"
  },
